      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">What a browser-based till is good for, and what it is not</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">This suits a single counter in a small shop: a catalogue of products, a cart, a GST receipt and a printer. It is genuinely useful because it costs nothing, needs no installation, works on a cheap tablet, and keeps billing through an internet outage. Be clear about the limits before you rely on it. Data lives in that browser on that device, so it does not sync between two counters, there is no central reporting across outlets, and clearing browser data removes your catalogue. If you need multi-terminal stock control, staff accounts or consolidated reporting, you need a proper POS system with a server behind it. For a single till that needs to start working this afternoon, this is a reasonable answer.</p>
